  "query_preview": "/Through Tuesday/...\nIssued at 256 PM MDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\nTemperatures across our entire CWA are currently in the 60s and 70s, \nwith even cooler readings at mountain peaks and ridges, a reminder \nthat fall is just around the corner. However, the monsoon is not \nover just yet, with daily chances for showers and storms persisting \nthrough at least Saturday. Rain chances today remain very low (<20%) \nfor the Denver metro and east CO plains given the cooler \ntemperatures and limited instability as we sit in a post-frontal \nairmass. The high terrain will have a better chance for showers and \nstorms late this afternoon and evening, although coverage will be \nscattered to isolated in nature. \n\nAt the upper levels, a closed low pressure system over northern \nCalifornia and slight ridging to our northwest will turn flow aloft \nsouth-southwesterly by tomorrow afternoon, which will increase \nmonsoonal moisture advection. We expect shower coverage to increase \ntomorrow afternoon and evening, with widespread shower development \nover the mountains as precipitable water (PW) rises to values that \nare greater than the 90th percentile of climatology. Temperatures \nover the Denver metro will still have a chance to climb into the \n80s before cloud cover increases late in the afternoon. \n\nBy Thursday morning, a disturbance tracks through Arizona and onto \nsouthern Colorado. As the previous AFD stated, the arrival of this \ndisturbance will be key in the coverage and intensity of rainfall on \nThursday. An arrival by early afternoon would coincide with peak \ninstability and moisture, which would imply a better chance for \nwidespread showers and storms over both mountains and east CO \nplains, including the Denver metro. With this scenario, the threat \nfor localized heavy rainfall and flash flooding would also be \nhigher. Looking at flash flooding parameters, moisture alone looks \nquite impressive, with PW values as high as 5 standard deviations \nabove normal for the time of year. High-res model guidance also \nshows warm cloud layer depths as deep as 4,000-7,000 ft, and storm \nmotions around 20KT. With these ingredients in mind, at least some \nlocalized flash flooding seems possible, although the spatial \nfootprint of the heaviest precipitation remains unclear. \n\nFor Friday, moisture remains about 100-150% of normal, and a weak \nupper level trough begins to track eastwards providing yet another \npush for showers and storms. Depending on the evolution of this \ntrough and another shortwave to the south over the Four Corners, \nSaturday could also be an active day, particularly for the high \nterrain as moisture remains well above normal through Saturday \nevening. Temperatures will remain slightly below normal Thursday \nthrough Tuesday, with afternoon highs in the mid 70s for low \nelevations, and 50s-60s for the mountains. Long-range model\nensembles show a series of upper level troughs and disturbances \ntracking through the western United States, which introduces quite\na bit of uncertainty next week.",
